{
  #standard-article(
  title: [Weekly Review],
  author: [Harper’s Magazine],
  source-name: [Harper's Magazine],
  images: (),
  paragraphs: (
  [The United States and Israel launched missile strikes against Iran, hitting military facilities, nuclear sites, ballistic-missile sites, and a girls’ school, where at least 165 people, the vast majority of them children, were killed. 1 2 3 Iran’s supreme leader was assassinated in the operation, news of which was confirmed by a post to his official X account depicting a robed warrior wielding a flaming sword. 4 “At some point they’ll be calling me to ask who I’d like,” the American president said when questioned about the supreme leader’s replacement. “I’m only being a little sarcastic.” 5 The president suggested that he launched the strikes, code-named Operation Epic Fury, partially owing to Iran’s interference in his unsuccessful bid for the presidency in 2020, having made similar claims about Venezuela’s government after the capture of that country’s president earlier this year. 6 “Feels like déjà vu,” an Iraq War veteran said of Epic Fury. 7 “We have to avoid overlearning the lessons of the past,” said the vice president. “Just because one president screwed up a military conflict doesn’t mean we can never engage in military conflict again.” 8 The American president said it was “likely” that U. S. service members would die during the ongoing conflict in the region; Israel bombed southern Lebanon, killing at least thirty-one people; the Houthi militia in Yemen indicated that it would resume its attacks on U. S. and Israeli ships in the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden; three U. S. fighter jets were shot down by friendly fire in Kuwait; the managing director of a British travel insurance agency explained to stranded United Kingdom nationals that most policies do not cover war or “large-scale conflict events”; and a luxury hotel in Dubai was engulfed in flames after the United Arab Emirates intercepted an Iranian missile. 9 10 11 12 13 14 At the Dubai Tennis Championships, the doubles final proceeded, and the Iranian soccer chief said his country’s team “cannot look forward to the World Cup with hope.” 15 16 “We didn’t sleep a wink all night,” said the Italian rapper BigMama, whose flight from the Maldives was rerouted to the Emirati desert. 17 Seventy-one minutes before news broke that the United States and Israel had struck Iran, a Polymarket user called “Magamyman” placed an \$87,000 bet on the strikes’ taking place, in turn netting more than half a million dollars. 18],
  [The American president banned government agencies from using products designed by the artificial-intelligence company Anthropic, and the secretary of war designated the company a “supply chain risk to national security” following the breakdown of negotiations regarding the use of the company’s products to create fully autonomous weapons systems and the means for the mass-surveillance of American citizens. 19 20 21 Around five hours later, OpenAI, which has received some \$1 billion in funding from a venture-capital firm operated by the brother of the president’s son-in-law and whose cofounder donated \$25 million to a pro-Trump super PAC earlier this year, announced it had signed a contract with the government. 22 23 24 25 It was reported that the U. S. military used Anthropic’s AI tool, Claude, in its strikes on Iran after the ban on the company had taken effect; and Claude overtook ChatGPT, OpenAI’s product, as the bestselling chatbot on Apple’s App Store. 26 27 The Trump Administration announced that it would withhold \$259 million in Medicaid funds from Minnesota, with the vice president saying that he was “quite confident that we have the authority to do this”; and the secretary of war accused the youth organization Scouting America, once known as the Boy Scouts of America, of being “anti-American.” 28 29 “I’ve heard a lot of dumb stuff,” a Republican congressman said in response, “but this is up there.” 30],
  [The Bolivian military dispersed a mob of hundreds from the crash site of an Air Force plane that had been carrying newly printed banknotes, and the American military used a laser to shoot down a drone operated by the country’s own Border Patrol. 31 32 The English rock band Radiohead demanded that Immigration and Customs Enforcement remove one of the band’s songs from a promotional video, telling the “amateurs” at the agency to “go fuck yourselves”; and Chicago residents voted to name a municipal snowplow “Abolish ICE.” 33 34 An Australian grocery chain tinkered with its AI assistant after the technology repeatedly complained about its mother; Burger King announced that it would debut an AI agent, to be programmed into workers’ headsets, that will collect data on their “friendliness”; and a Spanish software engineer accidentally hacked into seven thousand vacuum cleaners. 35 36 37 It was reported that callers to the Washington State Department of Licensing who had selected the Spanish-language service were patched through to an AI speaking Spanish-accented English. 38 The pope urged priests not to use AI when writing their homilies. 39 A woman who is on probation for sneaking onto a flight to Paris last year was arrested for stowing away on a flight to Milan. 40 A Turkish soccer match was paused so that a player could perform CPR on a seagull that had been struck by a kicked ball. 41 “We missed out on the championship,” said the player, “but helping save a life is a good thing.” 42 —Megan Evershed],
  [The post Weekly Review appeared first on Harper's Magazine .],
),
